external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/test_*
external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*
# Sources are NEVER artifacts. The two rules above target the extensionless compiled binaries,
# but they matched test_*.cpp too, and the defence was a hand-maintained list of per-file
# negations -- so every new test had to be enumerated by hand, and forgetting was SILENT: the file
# builds, links and passes locally while never reaching CI or a reviewer. A contract that exists
# only on one disk is worse than no contract. Negating by EXTENSION removes the list and the
# failure mode together; the extensionless binaries stay ignored.
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/test_*.cpp
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/test_*.h
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.cpp
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.h
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.f90
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.F90
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.py
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.sh
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.txt
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.md
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/test_*.golden
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/check_core_archive.sh
!external/libtorch_wrf/sdirk3/tests/lint_from_blob_baseline.txt
tools/fseeko_test
tools/registry

# OS/editor noise
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Compiler-generated header dependencies. The Makefile creates these with -MMD and deletes
# them in `clean`, and they embed absolute local toolchain paths, so tracking them makes
# `make clean` delete tracked files and buries reviews under ~210k lines of machine output.
*.d

# Autogenerated by ./compile from Registry/Registry.EM (its own first line says so). The tracked
# copy had gone stale -- it said `include Registry.sdirk3` while only Registry.SDIRK3 exists --
# and compile only regenerates when Registry.EM is NEWER, so a `git checkout` of the stale copy
# makes compile keep it. Invisible on case-insensitive macOS, unbuildable on Linux.
/Registry/Registry

# Generated by ./configure from arch/configure.defaults + arch/postamble, and machine-specific
# (5 /opt/homebrew toolchain paths). The tracked copy had gone stale on a MEASURED fix: it still
# said `LD = $(FC)` while its generators carry `LD = g++` for PR 9C.3, whose own comment records
# that gfortran cannot unwind C++ exceptions on this toolchain. Because compile:7 only checks that
# the file EXISTS, tracking it let a fresh clone skip `./configure` and silently rebuild with the
# defect that fix removes. Untracked, that path fails loudly with "You must run the 'configure'
# script first".
/configure.wrf
